

1. Three Daily Apps and Their ML Features

| App           | ML Feature                                                                  | Type of Learning          |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| **Instagram** | Suggests posts/reels based on your interests and past activity.             | Supervised Learning       |
| **Zomato**    | Suggests restaurants or dishes based on your previous orders and ratings.   | Supervised Learning       |
| **Flipkart**  | Groups and recommends products similar those you searched/purchased.        | Unsupervised Learning     |


Note: Real-world recommendation systems often combine supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning. These are simplified examples for clarity.

2. Classify the Scenarios

Netflix recommending movies based on watch history → Supervised Learning  
Learns from labeled data (ratings, watch history) to predict preferences.

Spotify grouping similar songs into playlists → Unsupervised Learning  
Clusters songs by audio features without predefined labels.

Self-driving car learning to park by trial and error → Reinforcement Learning  
Learns through actions and feedback (rewards/penalties).

3. Basic ML Workflow (Swiggy Example)

Data → Preprocessing → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment

Data: Collect order history, restaurant details, ratings, delivery times.

Preprocessing: Handle missing values, normalize delivery time data.

Modeling: Train ML model to predict delivery time or recommend restaurants.

Evaluation: Measure accuracy using test datasets.

Deployment: Integrate the model into the Swiggy app for real users.

